  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nAn broad upper-level trough is currently located over the \neastern CONUS as a surface low is deepening over the Mid- \nAtlantic. A mid-level shortwave is starting to spread over the\nsouthern parts of the region, leading to developing\nprecipitation along an around a west to east oriented stationary\nfront. Rain will continue to increase in coverage and intensity\nthrough the rest of today across the southern half of New\nHampshire and portions of western Maine (mainly the southwest)\nas the surface low starts to lift into southern New England and\neventually out into the Atlantic. Some of the guidance in\nprevious days had this low moving up into the Gulf of Maine but\nwe seem to have converged on a farther south solution which will\nlikely keep eventual QPF totals suppressed. This makes sense as\nthe system is obviously fighting a very dry airmass to the\nnorth.\n\nStill, as the low advances north and the shortwave influence\nspreads across southern New Hampshire and southwest Maine, we\nwill see precipitation coverage and intensity increase in during\nthe evening and overnight hours before moving out quickly on \nFriday. Looking at potential QPF totals, NBM 24-hour \nprobabilities suggest that the best chances for a half inch of \nliquid will be over southwest and south central New Hampshire. \nHere, the latest NBM expects a 40 to 80 percent chance of at \nleast a half inch (probabilities decrease quickly moving east \nand north). Decreasing the threshold to a quarter inch, the \nmoderate to high probabilities expand into southeast New \nHampshire and extreme southwest Maine. So, for most locations \nacross the south, expect a broad quarter to half inch of liquid \nwith amounts decreasing rapidly moving north.\n\nAs far as frozen precipitation goes, we have see a few reports\nof light ice pellets early this afternoon so wouldn't be\nsurprised if some of that activity continues through the rest of\nthe afternoon as the atmosphere moistens. We will see snow\nstarting to mix in some location through the afternoon and some\nlocations may change to all snow tonight, especially at\nelevations over 1000 feet across southern New Hampshire (the\nMonadnocks sill appear to be the best chances for a couple\ninches of accumulation). Upslope precipitation could lead to \nsome minor accumulations along and south of the White Mountains \nas well. NBM probabilities have decreased markedly for \naccumulations outside of the higher terrain, but many locations\nacross the south could still see their first flakes of the\nseason and/or a brief dusting. \n\nAfter the bulk of steady precipitation moves out early on\nFriday, we could still see a few rain/snow showers hanging\naround over the mountains. Saturday looks mainly dry with a few\nmore hit or miss shower across the higher terrain but an upper\nlow dives down from east central Quebec Saturday night into\nSunday morning, which could lead to better coverage of showery\nactivity. High pressure then moves in at the end of the weekend\nand into next week, which will keep the forecast dry with\nmoderating temperatures.",
